x First thing we're going to do is reproduce and clean up the preprocessing
x pipeline. The current one is janky and has no tests or anything to keep it out
x of trouble.
x 
x Here's what I think happens:
x 
x - Certain image data layers are rendered as tiles for dynamic fetching. This includes the WAC, Sectionals, TACs, IFR High & Low, a few specialty charts (like the
x Portland inset and the Grand Canyon). I assume these all use the standard
x mercator quadtree. (whenever I make assumptions, correct me if they're wrong!)
x 
x - Other image data are small enough that they're standalone, like approach plates,
x af/d ("chart supplement" as the kids call them these days). Some of these
x have georeferencing data, but rather than squish the chart to a standard geo
x layout, the client goes the opposite direction from the tiling approach: it
x renders the chart roughly in its original pixels, and draws a little airplane
x by mapping geo coordinates to plate coordinates.
x     - Some of the georeference data is collected by the Avare folks. Can we
x     fetch it from a database they provide?
x 
x 
x We're going to write our preprocessor in Rust.
x It will have an intelligent fetching-and-caching layer so we're not hitting the
x upstream image sources over-and-over during testing.
x It will organize its work in a way that reveals a lot of parallelism, since the
x tile rendering task is pretty parallelizable. We want to get a lot of benefit
x from the 20 cores on my server.
x 
x One thing we should do is run a copy of the original preprocessor and collect
x logs of all the tiles it made, and figure out how to use that info to write
x tests to ensure that our version doesn't introduce new gaps.
x 
x We can do some quick prototypes here, but once we really start chewing through
x data, we'll move that work onto a container on my server to exploit its many
x cores. If you think we can exploit a GPU, I can give you one of those too.
x 
x I think our first cut should reproduce the layout & data format of the original
x tool directly, so we can compare their output straight across and confirm
x that the tiles match: all the same tiles selected, and sample a bunch to confirm
x they basically match visually.
x 
x I understand the original code is a hodge-podge of scripts. I'd like our thing
x to be as clean and orderly as possible for maintenance going forward.
x 
x Considering what you've read already and the goals I've stated here, make a
x plan and discuss.

