x I think we need to change what valid means. Users are gonna construct 1-waypoint flight plans;
x that's how you get from an empty flight plan to a 2-waypoint flight plan.
x Gut instinct: No matter the flight plan contents,
x - Every top-level object (waypoint, airway block, procedure block) can be removed or reordered.
x - Such reordering may cause a flight plan to start with an airway, or end with an airway, or
x only have an airway, or have an airway before an airway. I don't care. There are still
x a clearly defined set of legs: materialize all the waypoints on the airways, and the legs
x are the pairwise edges.
x This route is "Let the user move their legos; we'll find some sort of meaning for it afterward."
x Can we make this work? What are some corner cases I need to pass judgment on?

x dedupe is yes. If the airway ends with UBG and UBG is also a top-level waypoint, remove
x the trailing UBG atom from the airway's materialization. But the airway spec is still
x V23(-, UBG), so that if the flight plan changes around it, the terminal UBG atom will pop
x into exstince. This approach just keeps there from being a confusing duplicate on the FP
x view, or from having a 0-length active leg.
