x A big change indeed, but an important one.

Interactions between containers (airways/procedures) and top-level waypoints:
Reorder of top-level waypoints should skip over procedures -- that is, we're
reordiring among the list of top-level things.
Reorder is not available inside containers.

Removing the first or last point of an airway should be allowed, because it's
well-defined: just redefine the airway spec to start one stop later / end earlier.
If an airway has degenerated to V23(PAE,PAE) (which materializes to PAE), then
removing the waypoint deletes the container, too.

Should we allow other removing inside a container? Hmm, but how would we represent this intent?
With tombstones? Eewww. No, let's defer this idea. Maybe we'll add a "flatten" action
the user can do if they really must break a procedure. But not for now.

Write tests for this stuff.


"- guidance geometry - the flight-plan core now knows more semantics, but it still mostly resolves to fix-to-fix pathing plus discontinuities"

I think you're talking about two things here.
One is, given a current position ("situation"), what should we display on the CDI?

The second is, given the current leg (and perhaps an understanding of the next leg / discontinuity),
how do we determine when we've completed the leg and should either advance to the next leg or
suspend guidance? I think I have a good intuition here:
- If the current leg is a straight segment and is the last (or before a
  discontinuity), then we're "done" when we pass the line normal to the leg
that intercepts the end waypoint.
- If the current leg is a straight segment and so is the next, then we're "done"
when we pass the line that bisects the angle between the two segments.
- If either leg is an arc, we can model it as a segment that meets at the common waypoint
and leaves tangent to the arc.
Did I miss anything else?

I'd love it if we had a test suite of different leg-completion-detection cases.
A matrix of interesting combinations -- different leg types, different join angles.
And for each cell, a selection of points in space on and near the lines, and some quite
off the intended track, and the intended match.
And then (this is the hooman part) a way to draw a sample chart, laid over sectional
tiles (HTML is fine!), with the two legs drawn in magenta & grey, and then draw the boundary
of the half-plane (or whatever) that defines "still in the leg" vs "passed into next leg".
And the test case dots plotted in their respective locations in red or white depending
on whether they're "in" or "out."
And then I can flip through a bunch of these "screenshots" (or HTML pages) and
see if they look about right.

x The PLN/Waypoint/"charts" button should say "Plates", and take you to the folder
x for that airport.
x 
x Procedure select entry tray doesn't need a "back" button.

x Procedures should have a menu tray option "change procedure" analogous to "change airway"

x These two are probably related:
